apiVersion: 1

{# Grafana file-provisioned alerting — the contact point (#139).

   This is an Ansible TEMPLATE, not a static file under files/data/, because the
   destination address is a vault value and must not be committed. That has one
   consequence the role's tasks/main.yml has to handle and which must not be
   undone: the "Deploy Grafana provisioning" synchronize runs with delete:true
   over the same directory, so it carries
   `--exclude=alerting/contact-points.yaml`. Remove that exclude and the rsync
   deletes this file on every run, the template writes it back, and Grafana
   restarts on every deploy forever.

   Grafana does interpolate ${ENV} inside DATASOURCE provisioning files, which is
   how datasources.yaml gets its credentials. That is not documented for alerting
   provisioning, so this templates the value in rather than assuming.

   `addresses` below is vault_watchtower_email_to — the host's shared Gmail relay,
   see the comment block in templates/env.j2. Deliberately reusing watchtower's
   credentials rather than adding a second copy of the same Gmail app password to
   the vault. Keep every Jinja comment OUTSIDE the mapping below: a Jinja comment
   block sitting between `settings:` and its first key leaves the block's own
   indentation in the rendered output, which is valid YAML today only because
   `settings` has exactly one key. (And never write a literal comment-close
   sequence inside one of these blocks — it terminates the comment early and the
   rest of the prose renders into the file. Yes, that happened.)

   #201 — THE DRILL MARKER IS WIRED IN HERE. Every email receiver below carries a
   `subject:` and every telegram receiver a `message:`, both pointing at the
   templates provisioned by
   files/data/grafana/provisioning/alerting/notification-templates.yaml. Those
   are GO template braces, so each one sits inside a Jinja raw block — without it
   Jinja would try to resolve `template` as a variable and the render would fail
   (loudly, which is the good case) or emit an empty string (the boot-fatal
   case). The lines are YAML comments, not Jinja comment blocks, precisely
   because of the indentation trap described above.

   Email gets `subject:` only: Grafana renders the email BODY from its own HTML
   template, and the subject is what a phone shows at 3am. Telegram has no
   subject, so the marker goes at the front of the message. All four receivers
   are covered — homelab-email-primary, homelab-critical-email,
   homelab-critical-telegram, homelab-telegram-primary — and tasks/main.yml
   asserts that every template name referenced here is actually defined by that
   file, because a dangling `template "..."` reference is a render error at
   NOTIFY time, i.e. an alert that fires and is never delivered. #}
contactPoints:
  - orgId: 1
    name: homelab-email
    receivers:
      - uid: homelab-email-primary
        type: email
        settings:
          addresses: {{ vault_watchtower_email_to }}
          # #201: renders the [DRILL] marker ahead of Grafana's default.title.
          subject: '{% raw %}{{ template "homelab.subject" . }}{% endraw %}'
        disableResolveMessage: false

{# homelab-critical (#152): the both-channels receiver the severity=critical route
   targets. When Telegram is disabled it degrades to email-only — exactly the
   coverage #139 shipped — so the policy template can reference it
   UNCONDITIONALLY. That shape matters: a route pointing at a receiver that does
   not exist is a FATAL Grafana provisioning error, and this is what keeps the
   route from ever dangling as the flag flips. #}
  - orgId: 1
    name: homelab-critical
    receivers:
      - uid: homelab-critical-email
        type: email
        settings:
          addresses: {{ vault_watchtower_email_to }}
          # #201: renders the [DRILL] marker ahead of Grafana's default.title.
          subject: '{% raw %}{{ template "homelab.subject" . }}{% endraw %}'
        disableResolveMessage: false
{% if observability_alert_telegram | bool %}
      - uid: homelab-critical-telegram
        type: telegram
        settings:
          bottoken: "{{ vault_grafana_telegram_bot_token }}"
          chatid: "{{ vault_grafana_telegram_chat_id }}"
          # #201: telegram has no subject, so the marker leads the message.
          message: '{% raw %}{{ template "homelab.message" . }}{% endraw %}'
        disableResolveMessage: false
{% endif %}
{% if observability_alert_telegram | bool %}
{# Telegram-ONLY receiver, for the one alert that must not depend on email:
   obs-alert-delivery-failing. #152's core argument — a rule that emails you to
   say email is broken is not a control. Rendered only when the channel exists;
   until then that rule falls through to the root policy (email), which is
   transitional and stated in the rule's own comment.

   `chatid` is quoted here AND the vault value is itself a quoted string, on
   purpose: an unquoted YAML scalar parses as an INTEGER and Grafana's contact
   point wants the textual form. Key names are `bottoken`/`chatid`, Grafana's own
   provisioning spelling — verified against /api/v1/provisioning/contact-points
   after the apply, because a wrong key here is a provisioning error and the
   role's /api/health gate is what catches it. #}
  - orgId: 1
    name: homelab-telegram
    receivers:
      - uid: homelab-telegram-primary
        type: telegram
        settings:
          bottoken: "{{ vault_grafana_telegram_bot_token }}"
          chatid: "{{ vault_grafana_telegram_chat_id }}"
          # #201: telegram has no subject, so the marker leads the message.
          message: '{% raw %}{{ template "homelab.message" . }}{% endraw %}'
        disableResolveMessage: false
{% endif %}
